21:56:40 [[chatgpt-bridge]] chatgpt-bridge.mcp INFO Tool call: name=ask_chatgpt args={'prompt': '## STEP 1 COMPLETE — Inspection Results\n\n### Git State\n9 modified files, 1 untracked (`server/port_cleanup.py`), `.runtime/` directory.\n\n### Startup Control Flow (verified from source)\n1. `main()` → `setup_logging()` → creates `ServerState` → creates `WebSocketServer` + `MCPServer`\n2. `ws_server.start()` → `cleanup_stale_listener(PORT=8765, timeout=2.0)` → `connect()` → `write_pid_file(PORT)`\n3. `start_token_http_server()` on port 8766\n4. `asyncio.create_task(mcp_server.run())` → starts MCP stdin loop\n5. Enters `while not stop_event.is_set()` loop with `asyncio.wait([stop_task, mcp_task], return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)`\n6. On stop/EOF: cancels `_cleanup_task`, cancels `mcp_task`, closes WS server, closes token HTTP server\n\n### stdout/stderr Analysis\n- `server/main.py:42` — `logging.StreamHandler(sys.stderr)` ✅ correct for MCP\n- `server/mcp_server.py:443-444` — `sys.stdout.write(line)` + `sys.stdout.flush()` — intentional MCP protocol writer\n- `server/__main__.py:9` — `print(..., file=sys.stderr)` ✅ correct\n- `server/port_cleanup.py` — has `print()` calls but these are only used when run as a standalone CLI, not imported during normal startup\n- No logging leaks to stdout. No `print()` calls in the critical startup path.\n\n### Key Code Paths\n- **`mcp_server.run()`**: Tries `loop.add_reader()` first (TTY), falls back to `run_in_executor` (pipes). Both paths loop until cancelled/EOF.\n- **`main()` lifecycle**: Uses `asyncio.wait([stop_task, mcp_task], return_when=FIRST_COMPLETED)` — no repeated `wait_for` on the MCP task itself.\n- **Signal handlers**: SIGINT/SIGTERM set `stop_event`, which triggers the shutdown sequence.\n\n### Suspicious Items\n1. `__main__.py` changed from `from server.main import main` to `from .main import main` — both should work with `python -m server` but worth noting\n2. `cleanup_stale_listener` runs before binding — if it misidentifies the new process as stale, it could kill it\n3. No `finally` block — if an exception escapes the `try` block, it goes to the outer `except Exception` which calls `logger.exception` and `raise`\n4. The `sys.exit(0)` at the end only triggers for pipe stdin\n\nNo obvious crash cause found. The server should start and respond to initialize. Ready for Step 2.\n', 'timeout': 300000}
